import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/*
We remove one simple path from a tree.
After removal, each remaining connected component is attached to exactly one vertex on the removed path
(because in a tree, every remaining vertex reaches the path through a unique first path vertex).

For a chosen path P, the remaining components are exactly:
for each vertex x on P, for each neighbor y of x not on P, the subtree/component reached through edge (x,y).
We want to maximize how many such components have size >= K.

Tree DP idea:
For every directed edge p -> v, define downSize[v wrt p] = size of the component containing v after removing edge (p,v).
Then component through neighbor y of x has known size depending on orientation:
- if y is child of x in rooted tree: subtreeSize[y]
- if y is parent of x: N - subtreeSize[x]

Now define contribution of a vertex x under path-state:
- endpoint state at x with previous path neighbor pre:
    count of neighbors of x except pre whose component size >= K
- internal state at x with two path neighbors pre and nxt:
    count of neighbors of x except pre,nxt whose component size >= K

If we build path incrementally, when extending from pre -> x -> y:
